{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And it was, as he drew back his hand.</b> Four times the word יָד is written here, corresponding to four bans-of-dedication (חֲרָמוֹת) that Achan, who came forth from him, misappropriated. This matter is alluded to here to indicate that because Achan came forth from him, therefore he would not succeed to the kingship — for ״a king by judgment establishes the land, but a man of dedications destroys it״ (Proverbs 29:4), for if he inclines after gain he will come to accept bribes. Therefore he drew back his hand, so that the birthright and the kingship should not be his." "And now there is room for me to explain these four mentions of יָד, which carry an allusion to a man who covets money and does not depart from this world with even half his desire in his hand — ״a maneh is what the coveter desires, two hundred״ and so forth. Now every man has two hands, with which he labors and toils to attain the fullness of his two fists by his strength and the power of his hand, according to what his hand can reach. If so, everything within his capacity is grasped by his two hands; and if he is not satisfied with what Elohim has graced him — the fullness of his two fists — then he covets yet another fullness of his two fists, that is, the labor of four hands, double what is grasped by his two hands. This is precisely the measure of the owner of a maneh who covets two hundred, that is, double what he has already grasped with his two hands.\n\nAnd everyone who covets money is not a man of righteousness; therefore he will not succeed to the kingship, because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 49a) that in the merit of David rendering justice to this one and righteousness to that one, Joab would prevail in battle. Therefore it is said of Judah (Deuteronomy 33:7) ״his hands — sufficient (רַב) to him״: it should have said ״they are sufficient (יִרְבּוּ) to him,״ for ״his hands״ is plural in meaning while ״רַב״ is singular. Rather, it teaches that he did not covet the money of two additional hands, for his two hands are רַב לוֹ — sufficient for him. For ״רַב״ serves in the sense of ״sufficient (דַּי),״ as in ״רַב לָךְ אַל תּוֹסֶף״ (Deuteronomy 3:26). And this alludes that the labor of his two hands was sufficient for him, for he sought no more beyond them; therefore ״and an aid from his adversaries shall he be״ (Deuteronomy 33:7) — for this very contentment is the cause of righteousness, through whose merit enemies fall before him. A proof from Joab, and as it is written (Psalms 112:5) ״good is the man who grants and lends״ etc., ״his heart is steadfast, he shall not fear, until he sees his adversaries״ (there v. 8). And so it is concluded in Bava Kamma (17a) that one who engages in deeds of lovingkindness — his enemies fall before him slain.\n\nTherefore it is said that in the merit of ״his hands — sufficient to him״ — the labor grasped by his two hands is sufficient for him and he seeks no surplus, and thus he is a man of righteousness — therefore ״and an aid from his adversaries shall he be.״ And this is said of Judah who came forth through Perez.\n\nBut Zerah was not one of ״his hands — sufficient to him״; rather, he sought the labor of four hands and coveted money that was not fitting for him, as is said of Achan (Joshua 7:21) ״and I coveted them and I took them.״ Therefore his right hand was turned back — ״as he drew back his hand״ — for the judgment of the birthright and the kingship shall not be his, for every king needs victory over enemies, and he is not capable of this, as has been said."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:37.470155283Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}